        <h2>Network Protocols and Devices</h2>

        <p>Computer networking involves using various protocols and devices to transmit and exchange data between computers and other devices. Protocols govern the communication between devices, enabling them to understand and interpret the data they receive. Some examples of commonly used network protocols include TCP/IP, the primary protocol used on the Internet, and IPv6, the latest version of the Internet Protocol (IP).</p>

        <p>Network devices, such as routers and switches, connect and manage networks. Routers forward data packets between networks, while switches connect devices within a single network. Network devices can be configured to support various networking protocols and perform various functions, such as <a href="/communications-expert-witness/routing-switching-expert-witness/">routing, switching, and security</a>.</p>

        <p>Dr. Lavian has worked with <a href="/communications-expert-witness/pstn-voip-expert-witness/">telecommunications network protocols</a> and devices throughout his career, including designing and configuring network architectures at Nortel Networks and in his research at UC Berkeley. He provides technical consulting related to network protocols, devices, and architectures.</p>

        <div class="bordered-section">
          <h3>Computer networks technologies</h3>

          <p>Many technologies are used in modern computer networks, including:</p>

          <ul>
            <li><a href="/communications-expert-witness/networking-expert-witness/">Networking</a> hardware, such as routers, switches, and hubs, provide physical connections and data <a href="/communications-expert-witness/routing-switching-expert-witness/">routing</a> between devices.</li>
            <li>Network protocols, such as TCP/IP, provide the rules and standards for communication between devices on a network.</li>
            <li>Network security technologies, such as firewalls and encryption, protect against unauthorized access and data interception.</li>
            <li>Network management tools, which allow for the monitoring and control of network performance and resources.</li>
            <li>Network automation uses software and algorithms to automate network configuration and management tasks.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>These technologies work together to enable efficient and secure data transmission over networks, allowing for the development of complex applications and services, such as e-commerce, social media, and cloud computing. As the Internet continues to evolve, new technologies will be developed to meet the growing demands of users and applications.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <h3>Bandwidth Throttling &amp; Quality of Service</h3>
            <p>Dr. Lavian has expertise in data throttling, bandwidth throttling, traffic shaping, and Quality of Service (QoS) systems that prioritize network traffic. These technologies are central to patent disputes involving internet service providers, enterprise networks, and content delivery systems. His technical background includes Priority Queuing, Weighted Fair Queuing (WFQ), Differentiated Services (DiffServ), and Traffic Classification mechanisms used by ISPs and enterprise network operators.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Software-Defined Networking &amp; Cloud</h3>
            <p>Dr. Lavian's expertise includes Software-Defined Networking (SDN) architectures, OpenFlow protocol, network function virtualization (NFV), and cloud networking platforms including AWS VPC, Azure Virtual Network, and Google Cloud networking. He has been retained in patent cases involving SDN controllers, virtual network overlays, and programmable network infrastructure.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <h3>Network Architecture Design and Implementation Analysis</h3>
            <p>Dr. Lavian analyzes computer network architecture patents by examining how specific design choices &mdash; topology selection, protocol layering, routing policy configuration, and device interconnection methods &mdash; map to patent claim elements. His background designing network switch software at Nortel Networks and conducting network architecture research at UC Berkeley provides direct engineering familiarity with the architectural decisions that generate patent disputes. He evaluates both the theoretical principles documented in IEEE and IETF standards and the practical implementation in commercial enterprise and carrier network products including routers, switches, controllers, and network management systems.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>TCP/IP Networking and Protocol Stack Expertise</h3>
            <p>Many computer networking patent disputes turn on how specific TCP/IP protocol behaviors &mdash; TCP connection establishment and teardown, congestion control algorithms (e.g., TCP CUBIC, TCP Vegas), packet fragmentation and reassembly, socket-level handling, or HTTP/application-layer integration &mdash; implement specific claim elements. Dr. Lavian&rsquo;s 120+ patents include inventions covering protocol stack implementations across OSI layers, and his doctoral research at UC Berkeley specialized in network communications including TCP/IP. He provides claim construction and infringement analysis grounded in IETF RFC specifications, IEEE 802 standards, and commercial protocol stack implementations as documented in product source records and technical specifications.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

